DRAFT — NOT YET IN EFFECT

Licensing of the data shipped in this repository.

This file is a draft for owner review. It does not change, and is not changed by,
the project code's proposed MIT licence in `LICENSE.draft`. Code and data are
licensed separately and deliberately.

================================================================================
WHY TWO LICENCES, AND WHY SHARE-ALIKE DOES NOT REACH THE CODE
================================================================================

The measured affinity evidence in this repository comes from BindingDB, which
states its terms per data source rather than for the database as a whole:

  * data curated by BindingDB staff      Creative Commons Attribution 3.0
  * data imported from ChEMBL            Creative Commons Attribution-Share
                                         Alike 3.0 Unported

ChEMBL confirms the same for its own data: Attribution-Share Alike 3.0 Unported,
requiring attribution and that derivative works be shared under the same terms.

Share-alike attaches to adaptations of the licensed material. The project code is
not an adaptation of the data: it is independently authored software that reads
it. So the code stays MIT, and the share-alike obligation travels with the data
files and with anything derived from them. That separation is why there are two
licence files rather than one.

================================================================================
WHAT APPLIES TO WHAT
================================================================================

Row-level provenance was taken from BindingDB's own `Curation/DataSource` column,
which this repository preserves for every raw row. A file is listed under
CC BY-SA 3.0 where the compounds it references have measurements carrying
`Curation/DataSource = ChEMBL`.

--- CC BY-SA 3.0 Unported (attribution AND share-alike) --------------------

Source data — chemical structures:
  data/asof/m11g/recompute-compounds.json      InChIKey -> canonical SMILES
  reports/results/m10_cohort.json              600 members with canonical SMILES

Transformed data and identifiers:
  data/asof/m11d/examples.json
  data/asof/m11e/examples.json
  data/asof/m11e/worked-example-review.json
  data/asof/m11g/evaluation-entities.json
  data/asof/m11g/recurrence-agreement.json
  data/asof/m11g/reuse-compounds.json
  data/asof/m11h/run-20261005T134842Z/evaluation-pairs.jsonl
  reports/results/m11d_ambiguous_slots.jsonl
  reports/results/m11d_correction_candidates.jsonl
  reports/results/m11d_examples.json
  reports/results/m11e_examples.json
  reports/results/m11e_worked_example_review.json

Model predictions keyed by compound identifier:
  data/asof/m11h/run-20261005T134842Z/predictions.npz
      26,444 rows; the `pair` array carries 16,795 InChIKeys. Listed here
      because of those identifiers, not because the scores are ChEMBL content.
      Whether the scores themselves are an "Adaptation" under CC BY-SA 3.0 is
      an open question — see THE ONE OPEN
      QUESTION below.

Fitted statistics over predominantly ChEMBL-sourced rows:
  data/asof/m11h/run-20261005T134842Z/protein-transform.npz
      A 1x1280 mean, a 1x1280 scale and a fitted-row count: 2,560 summary
      statistics over ESM-2 embeddings of training proteins. The bytes carry no
      identifier, no structure, no sequence and no measured value.

      Listed here as a CONSERVATIVE PACKAGING DECISION, not as a legal
      conclusion that share-alike attaches. The reason is the population it
      summarises, which was measured rather than assumed: the transform's own
      `n_fitted` is 353,957, exactly the A-train pairs the partition record
      reports as supplied to fitting, and resolving those pairs against
      BindingDB's row-level `Curation/DataSource` gives

        exact-Ki records behind them        454,694
        ChEMBL-sourced records              304,963  (67.07%)
        pairs with >=1 ChEMBL-sourced row   251,811  (71.14%)
        train sequences touching ChEMBL      2,613 of 3,466  (75.4%)

      An earlier revision of this file placed the transform under MIT on the
      ground that it contains no identifier. That is true of the bytes and does
      not settle the question, because the file is a statistic over data that is
      roughly seven-tenths CC BY-SA 3.0. See THE ONE OPEN QUESTION below for what
      is and is not being decided here.

--- CC BY 3.0 (attribution) ------------------------------------------------

  data/asof/m11g/recompute-targets.json        13 protein sequences, no ChEMBL
                                               measurement rows
  reports/examples/query_target.fasta          one protein sequence (demo input)
  reports/results/m11d_identifier_conflicts.jsonl
                                               BindingDB record metadata;
                                               its one compound has no
                                               ChEMBL-sourced rows

--- No third-party data; covered by LICENSE.draft (MIT) --------------------

  All other tracked data and report files: counts, digests, metrics, cohort
