REQUIRED ATTRIBUTION
================================================================================

When redistributing any file listed above, carry the following.

BindingDB, for all measured affinity evidence:

  Liu T, Hwang L, Burley SK, Nitsche CI, Southan C, Walters WP, Gilson MK.
  BindingDB in 2024: a FAIR knowledgebase of protein-small molecule binding
  data. Nucleic Acids Research. 2025;53:D1633-D1644.

  Data curated by BindingDB staff: Creative Commons Attribution 3.0.
  https://www.bindingdb.org/

Snapshot A, the historical training snapshot:

  BindingDB archival deposit, 2026-01-01. DOI 10.6075/J0V40W61.
  UC San Diego Library Digital Collections.

Snapshot B: the locally pinned BindingDB 202609 rolling release. BindingDB
replaces the rolling file monthly and does not archive prior months at a stable
URL, so these exact bytes may not be re-acquirable by a third party; the SHA-256
pin in `src/seq2lead/ingest/sources.py` detects a substitution rather than
tolerating one. See `docs/RELEASE_SCOPE.md`.

ChEMBL, for the rows BindingDB attributes to it:

  ChEMBL, European Molecular Biology Laboratory - European Bioinformatics
  Institute. Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 3.0 Unported.
  https://www.ebi.ac.uk/chembl/

  Cite the specific ChEMBL release DOI where a release is identified. This
  repository does not record which ChEMBL release each imported row came from;
  BindingDB's export carries the source label but not the ChEMBL release
  identifier.

ESM-2, for the protein representations:

  facebook/esm2_t33_650M_UR50D, revision
  08e4846e537177426273712802403f7ba8261b6c, declared MIT. Neither the model
  code nor its weights are redistributed here.

Full licence texts: licenses/CC-BY-3.0.txt, licenses/CC-BY-SA-3.0.txt.

Those two files are a third class of their own, and the release inventory records
them as such rather than labelling them with the licence they contain. They are
Creative Commons' legal code, reproduced verbatim and unmodified:

  * They are shipped because CC BY 3.0 and CC BY-SA 3.0 section 4(a) each
    require that a copy of the licence, or its URI, accompany every copy of the
    Work distributed. Shipping the text is compliance, not redistribution of
    someone's content by choice.
  * The basis for reproducing them is Creative Commons' own policy: "Creative
    Commons makes the legal code of its licenses and the CC0 Public Domain
    Dedication available under the CC0 Public Domain Dedication, as well as the
    text of all of the notices and accompanying text on the license pages"
    (creativecommons.org/policies, "Legal text (we call this legal code) and
    Commons deeds"). So the inventory labels them CC0-1.0.
  * CC "reserves fully and unconditionally all trademark and branding rights",
    and states that if you change the text of a CC licence "you may no longer
    refer to it as a Creative Commons or CC license". The bytes are therefore
    reproduced exactly, with their digests recorded in licenses/README.md, and
    are never edited. Nothing in this repository uses CC branding to suggest
    Creative Commons endorses it.

================================================================================
HOW THE DATA WAS TRANSFORMED
================================================================================

Nothing shipped here is a verbatim copy of a BindingDB export. The chain:

 1. Ingest. Raw rows loaded verbatim into an immutable layer with a
    `source_release` row pinning name, version, SHA-256 and URL. Every raw
    payload is retained, including `Curation/DataSource`.
 2. Standardisation. RDKit 2026.03.6 fragment-parent standardisation; InChIKey
    assigned from the standardised parent; protein identity from the SHA-256 of
    the uppercased sequence.
 3. Endpoint build. Exact (`=`) and censored (`<`, `>`) records kept apart.
    `pair_regression` holds median pKi over exact records only; `pair_label`
    holds threshold labels from interval intersection. No censored value ever
    enters a median.
 4. Snapshot matching. January and September exports compared by counted
    multiset matching over measurement slots, producing unchanged, added,
    removed and correction-candidate populations with row locators.
 5. Features. ECFP4 radius 2, 2048 bits, chirality on, bit-packed to 256 bytes.
    ESM-2 mean-pooled over residues excluding special tokens.
 6. Fitting. Exact-Ki regression on January evidence alone; checkpoint selection
    on a reserved January validation split.
 7. Evaluation. Tie-aware per-target ranking metrics, macro-averaged over
    targets meeting a five-per-class floor.

The shipped identifier files are the *entity sets* and *aggregates* these steps
produced. `reuse-compounds.json`, for instance, is a mapping from InChIKey to an
internal surrogate id; it contains no structure and no measured value. The two
files that do ship structures are named explicitly above.

================================================================================
THE ONE OPEN QUESTION, AND WHAT WAS DECIDED ABOUT IT
================================================================================

The question is not answered by either provider's published terms:

  Are model predictions and fitted statistics derived from CC BY-SA 3.0 data
  themselves "Adaptations" under that licence, such that share-alike attaches
  to them?

What is affected: `predictions.npz` (scores for 26,444 pairs, keyed by 16,795
InChIKeys) and `protein-transform.npz` (2,560 summary statistics with no
identifiers, fitted over a population that is 71.14% ChEMBL-linked by pair).

Neither BindingDB's nor ChEMBL's terms address statistical or model-derived
outputs, and ChEMBL's CC BY-SA 3.0 defines "Adaptation" without contemplating a
fitted parameter. Missing wording is not permission and it is not prohibition. A
draft inquiry to both providers has been written and is held outside this
repository with the owner's working material; it has not been sent.

--- What is decided, and by whom -------------------------------------------

A PACKAGING DECISION (ours; made here; final for this release). Both files ship,
and both are labelled CC BY-SA 3.0 with the attribution block above. The release
inventory records the transform's label as an explicit `licence_exception` naming
this reasoning, so the choice is visible rather than implied by a bare label.

A LEGAL CONCLUSION (not ours; NOT made). Whether either file is an "Adaptation"
under CC BY-SA 3.0 section 1(a), and whether any database right is engaged by
extracting aggregate statistics, are questions this repository does not answer and
does not need to answer in order to ship correctly.

--- Why the conservative label, stated without evasion ---------------------

Not because obligations could be added later. The reason is that the error is
asymmetric and only one direction is recoverable:

  * Label CC BY-SA 3.0, and later learn share-alike does not attach -> the owner
    can relax the terms, because the only restriction being withdrawn is one we
    imposed.
  * Label MIT, and later learn share-alike does attach -> nothing can be
    withdrawn. Recipients already hold the file under terms we had no standing to
    offer, and the share-alike chain is broken for everyone downstream of them.

So the uncertainty IS treated as blocking for the permissive option. It is not
blocking for the release as a whole only because a label exists that is correct
under either answer, and that label is being used.

--- What is NOT being claimed ----------------------------------------------

Seq2Lead does not own the BindingDB or ChEMBL data and cannot license it to
anyone. The grant works in one direction only: to whatever extent Seq2Lead holds
any right in `predictions.npz` or `protein-transform.npz`, that right is licensed
under CC BY-SA 3.0, so the file is usable on those terms without anyone needing to
resolve the question above. Rights in the underlying measurements rest on
BindingDB's and ChEMBL's own grants, whose texts are reproduced in `licenses/` and
whose attribution is required above. Nothing here purports to grant, waive or
narrow a right held by BindingDB, ChEMBL, EMBL-EBI, UC San Diego or Meta.

--- The alternative that was considered and not taken ----------------------

Excluding `protein-transform.npz` from the release would also dispose of the
question. It was rejected because the file is load-bearing for reproduction, not
decorative: `scripts/asof/m11h_reproduce_integrity.py` copies it into the
self-contained run clone it builds, and the run manifest records its digest, so
removing it would weaken the tamper-detection reproduction in order to avoid a
labelling question that a label already answers. Excluding it remains available to
the owner at the cost of that coverage.

--- Owner approval required ------------------------------------------------

This draft is not in effect. The owner is asked to confirm the conservative
labelling of both model-derived artifacts, or to direct the alternative.
